  "preamble": "A rhotic [r] becomes the voiceless stop [t] by total assimilation to an adjacent [t]. The classic case is the Middle Indo-Aryan simplification of Sanskrit clusters: in Pali and the literary Prakrits the r of -tr- is absorbed by the stop, giving a geminate -tt- (Sanskrit putra > Pali, Prakrit putta 'son'; mitra > Pali mitta 'friend'; rātri > Prakrit ratti 'night'). Outside such clusters a direct change of a trill to a stop is not well attested.",
  "phoneticEffects": "Total Assimilation, Gemination, Cluster Simplification",
  "languageExamples": [
    {
      "language": "Pali",
      "languageFamily": "Indo-Aryan",
      "examples": [
        {
          "from": "Sanskrit पुत्र (putrá) 'son'",
          "to": "putta 'son'",
          "note": "Progressive assimilation of r to the preceding t in the cluster -tr-, giving a geminate."
        },
        {
          "from": "Sanskrit सूत्र (sū́tra) 'thread, rule'",
          "to": "sutta 'thread, string; sutra (discourse)'",
          "note": "Same assimilation of -tr- to -tt-, with the long vowel shortened before the geminate."
        },
        {
          "from": "Sanskrit मित्र (mitrá) 'friend'",
          "to": "mitta 'friend'",
          "note": "Same -tr- > -tt-."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "language": "Maharashtri Prakrit",
      "languageFamily": "Indo-Aryan",
      "examples": [
        {
          "from": "Sanskrit पुत्र (putrá) 'son'",
          "to": "𑀧𑀼𑀢𑁆𑀢 (putta) 'son'",
          "note": "Also in Shauraseni Prakrit."
        },
        {
          "from": "Sanskrit रात्रि (rā́tri) 'night'",
          "to": "𑀭𑀢𑁆𑀢𑀺 (ratti) 'night'",
          "note": "Long vowel shortened before the new geminate; the variant rāi reflects a different syllabification with simple t."
        }
      ]
